Subject: client: let the window settle before asking, and report stalls

Each window-size ask makes the Looking Glass IDD replug its monitor. A
burst of replugs, such as a monitor waking from sleep or a drag resize,
can hang the IDD: Windows stops sending frames and the window freezes on
its last picture, until the display adapter restarts.

The module now asks less often, and says when frames stop:

- An ask waits until the window's raw size, before the client's 500 ms
  message debounce, has held for 2 s (RESIZE_SETTLE_US). A burst of
  sizes makes one ask, for the last size, and a drag makes no ask until
  it stops. resize_raw records each raw size; a post older than the last
  change is ignored.
- Two asks are at least 4 s apart (RESIZE_GAP_US), for a new size or a
  retry. The first retry wait grows from 2 s to 4 s; 5, 10 and 20 s stay.
- resize_seen records every frame, not only format changes. With no
  frame for 10 s (RESIZE_STALL_US) while an ask is outstanding, counted
  from the later of the sequence's first ask and the last frame, or since
  a display session started, it logs
  "Resize: no frame from Windows for 10 s (asked for WxH)" or
  "(new session)", once. The stall lasts across sessions until a frame
  arrives, which logs "Resize: frames from Windows again". A still
  desktop also sends no frames, so a stall line can be a refused size
  and not a hang; whoever reads it must check before acting.
- Building with -DRESIZE_NO_SETTLE drops the settle and the gap and
  keeps the stall detector, for tests that force the hang.
- resize_test.c: cases whose timing changes on purpose say so, and new
  cases cover the settle, the gap and the stall.

No message is added and no message's format changes.

